WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
You've learned about how RNNs work and how they can be applied to problems like

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.340
name entity recognition as well as to language modeling.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:13.200
And you saw how backpropagation can be used to train an RNN.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.520
It turns out that one of the problems with the basic RNN algorithm is that it

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.580
runs into vanishing gradient problems.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.280
Let's discuss that and in the next few videos,

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.880
we'll talk about some solutions that will help to address this problem.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.700
So you've seen pictures of RNNs that look like this.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:34.640
And let's take a language modeling example.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:43.040
Let's say you see this sentence, the cat which already ate,

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.280
and maybe already ate a bunch of food that was delicious,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.000
was full.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.280
And so to be consistent is because cat is singular,

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.040
it should be the cat was.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:00.640
Whereas it was the cats which already ate a bunch of food,

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:06.720
was delicious and the apples and pears and so on were full.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:14.400
So to be consistent, it should be cat was or cats were.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.960
And this is one example of when language can have very long term dependencies

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.800
where a word that is much earlier can affect what needs to come much later

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.160
in the sentence.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.960
But it turns out the basic RNN we've seen so far is not very good at capturing

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.200
very long term dependencies.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.080
To explain why, you might remember from our early discussions of training

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.800
very deep neural networks that we talked about the vanishing gradient

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:43.360
problem.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.680
So this is a very, very deep neural network, say 100 layers or even much

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:54.000
deeper, then you would carry a forward prop from left to right, and then back prop.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.640
And we said that if this is a very deep neural network, then the gradient from this

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.440
output Y would have a very hard time propagating back to affect the weights

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:07.440
of these earlier layers to affect the computations in the earlier layers.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.240
And for an RNN, we have a similar problem.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:16.880
You have forward prop going from left to right, and then back prop going from right

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.000
to left.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.120
And it can be quite difficult because of the same vanishing gradient problem for

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.800
the outputs of the errors associated with the later time steps to affect the

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.400
computations that are earlier.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.760
And so in practice, what this means is it might be difficult to get the neural

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.720
network to realize that it needs to memorize.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.640
Did you see a singular noun or a plural noun so that later on in the sequence

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:49.120
could generate either was or were depending on whether it was singular or plural.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.520
And notice that in English, this stuff in the middle could be arbitrarily long,

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:53.760
right?

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:59.040
So you might need to memorize the singular plural for a very long time before

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:03.360
you get to use that bit of information.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:09.360
So because of this problem, the basic RNN model has many local influences,

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:17.920
meaning that the output Y hat 3 is mainly influenced by values close to Y hat 3.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.200
And a value here is mainly influenced by inputs that are somewhat close.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.760
And it's difficult for the output here to be strongly influenced by an input

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.520
that was very early in the sequence.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.520
And this is because whatever the output is, whether it is got it right,

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:37.520
this got it wrong, it's just very difficult for the error to backprop it

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.840
all the way to the beginning of the sequence, and therefore to modify how

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.200
the neural network is doing computations earlier in the sequence.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:49.280
So this is a weakness of the basic RNN algorithm,

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.600
one which we'll start to address in the next few videos.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.160
But if we don't address it, then it then RNNs tend not to be very good

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:00.480
at capturing long range dependencies.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.200
And even though this discussion has focused on vanishing gradients,

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.320
you remember when we're talking about very deep neural networks,

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.320
that we also talked about exploding gradients, where doing backprop,

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:14.800
the gradients should not just decrease exponentially,

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.480
they may also increase exponentially with the number of layers you go through.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.280
It turns out that vanishing gradients tends to be the bigger problem with training RNNs.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.360
Although when exploding gradients happens, it can be catastrophic

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:32.480
because the exponentially large gradients can cause your parameters to become so large

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.400
that your neural network parameters get really messed up.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:41.600
So it turns out that exploding gradients are easier to spot because the parameters just blow up,

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.200
and you might often see NANs or not in numbers,

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:51.200
meaning the results of numerical overflow in your neural network computation.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:57.200
And if you do see exploding gradients, one solution to that is to apply gradient clipping.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.680
And what that really means, all that means is look at your gradient vectors

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:06.240
and if it is bigger than some threshold,

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:09.280
rescale some of your gradient vectors so that it's not too big,

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.880
so that it's clipped according to some maximum value.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:18.800
So if you see exploding gradients, if your derivatives do explode or you see NANs,

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.560
just apply gradient clipping.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:26.080
And that's a relatively robust solution that will take care of exploding gradients.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:29.600
But vanishing gradients is much harder to solve,

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:32.480
and it will be the subject of the next few videos.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.560
So to summarize, in an earlier course, you saw how if you're training a very deep neural network,

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:43.040
you can run into vanishing gradient or exploding gradient problems with the derivative,

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:49.040
either decreases exponentially or grows exponentially as a function of the number of layers.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:53.600
And an RNN, say an RNN processing data over a thousand times steps,

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:59.520
or over 10,000 times steps, that's basically a 1,000 layer or like a 10,000 layer neural network.

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:02.880
And so it too runs into these types of problems.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.080
Exploding gradients, you could solve the badgers by just using gradient clipping,

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:11.200
but vanishing gradients will take more work to address.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:15.520
So what we'll do in the next video is talk about GRUs, or greater recurrent units,

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.240
which is a very effective solution for addressing the vanishing gradient problem

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:24.880
and will allow your neural network to capture much longer range dependencies.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:26.400
So let's go on to the next video.
